WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.570
Justice League has arrived, and finally united
six of DC Comics' flagship heroes.

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.720
Only together can they fight off an invasion
of violent parademons from the alien world

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.340
of Apokolips , and the evil Mother Box machinations
of… Darkseid's creepy uncle.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.180
Woven into the film's half-dozen converging
storylines were also a whole lot of little

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.400
Easter Eggs that called out to the Justice
League's rich comic book and movie history.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.650
Here are just a few.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.750
Party like it's 1989

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:32.390
For many Batman fans, the golden era of DC
movies began and ended with 1989's Batman,

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:37.540
directed by Tim Burton and scored by new wave
virtuoso and cinematic composer Danny Elfman.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:42.360
Elfman stepped away from the whole Batman
thing after 1992's Batman Returns, leaving

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.370
five indelible notes stuck in every Batman
fan's head forever.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:51.079
It took 25 years, but Elfman returned to score
Justice League, and he brought his old soundtrack

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:52.079
with him.

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:55.510
As Batman appears in front of Commissioner
Gordon on the rooftops of Gotham City, those

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.940
five notes can be heard again… and it's
pretty sweet.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.910
"Oh awesome that's the bat signal!

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.460
That's your… oh, shhh … sorry."

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:05.680
The Penguin

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:10.299
Even though Batman is usually only ever seen
fighting street toughs, Superman, and demon-bugs

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:15.140
from space, the DCEU drops hints about his
20 years of crimefighting in Gotham, from

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.670
the spraypainted Robin suit in his Batcave
to casual mentions of his past adventures.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:24.389
One especially great mention comes when Alfred
drops a bomb about exploding, wind-up penguins,

00:01:24.389 --> 00:01:25.920
which can only mean one thing.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.929
It's hard to imagine what a DCEU Penguin would
look like, but he'll probably have a lot of

00:01:29.929 --> 00:01:33.469
tattoos, daddy issues, and an incomprehensible
backstory.

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:37.729
Whatever happens, the DCEU needs to bring
back the bomb-birds.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:38.729
Janus

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:42.659
We also get a hint at another Batman villain
who goes by the name of Black Mask.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:47.079
As Batman battles a parademon across the skies
of Gotham, we see a bunch of different buildings,

00:01:47.079 --> 00:01:50.020
and one of them bears a large sign that says
Janus.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.829
The name refers to a few things in the DC
universe, but the most likely use here is

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:57.899
Janus Cosmetics, a company once run by the
psychotic Black Mask.

00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:02.159
Wayne Enterprises ends up purchasing Janus
Cosmetics and leaving Roman without a job,

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:05.990
which causes the murderous magnate to take
on the villainous mantle of Black Mask and

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.849
swear revenge on Bruce Wayne.

00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:10.340
At least it's not Kite Man, right?

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.510
Green Lantern

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:15.689
The schedule for the DC movie universe has
changed shape more times than Plastic Man,

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:20.430
but one thing that's been more or less consistent
is that the DCEU would include the Green Lantern

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.900
Corps, in one way or another.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.760
As of now, they'll be getting their own movie
in 2020, but no one really expected to see

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.410
a Lantern in Justice League.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.680
Still, at least one of the dudes appears during
the historic battle between the armies of

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.859
Apokolips and the collected might Earth's
heroes.

00:02:33.859 --> 00:02:38.430
It's a quick shout-out to future DCEU films,
and places the Corps on Earth at least once

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:42.780
during their billion-year history patrolling
the cosmos for bad business.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:43.810
New Gods

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.989
Leave it to horn-headed villain Steppenwolf
to literally drop the name of his first appearance

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:52.769
comic in one of his many speeches about how
the Old Gods are just a bunch of dead losers,

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:55.390
so it's up to the "New Gods" to set the universe
up right.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:00.010
Of course, Steppenwolf first appeared in New
Gods #7, so the guy is anything but subtle.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:03.969
The phrase "New Gods" refers to the entire
pantheon of heroes and villains devised by

00:03:03.969 --> 00:03:08.190
Jack Kirby, a bunch of super-powerful aliens
who split their residence between the warring

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.319
worlds of New Genesis and Apokolips.

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:14.609
Just how many of these weirdos will appear
in the DCEU is anyone's guess, but if DC knows

00:03:14.609 --> 00:03:17.590
what's good for them, we'll be seeing Granny
Goodness next.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:18.590
Right?

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.090
"You naughty little monkey!"

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.459
Superman v Flash

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:27.440
Who's faster: The Flash, master of the speed
force, or Superman, the alien who can do just

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.260
about anything except carry a playable video
game?

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.860
"Gimme five big guy! …too slow!"

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:39.060
The two heroes' comparable speed is glimpsed
briefly during the final fight against Steppenwolf,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.799
but Justice League's mid-credit scene is a
direct tribute to the many, many times that

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:46.920
Superman and Flash engaged in a friendly competition
to see who was fastest.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:47.920
Who wins?

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.340
You'll just have to check out Superman #199
to find out.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:54.120
Or Flash #175.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.329
Or Adventures of Superman #463.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:04:00.800
Or a dozen other comics that apparently never
definitively settled the score.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.349
Aren't there people who need saving somewhere?

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:04.519
Too many leagues

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:08.280
By the end of Justice League, both Bruce Wayne
and Lex Luthor are putting together their

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.280
teams.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.540
While neither one is outright named, the implications
are obvious.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:16.590
Bruce is making plans to set up a table with
six chairs… or maybe more.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.549
Bruce's hideout can only mean one thing.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:21.260
"Meanwhile, at the Hall of Justice."

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.180
The Hall pretty consistently features a table
at which all of the members of the League

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.810
make all of the big decisions, like how they're
going to beat the newest evil, or if they

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.139
should order from Quiznos or Subway.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.900
"We love the subs."

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:34.900
"Subway."

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:36.979
Tough choice.

00:04:36.979 --> 00:04:41.120
Meanwhile, an escaped Lex Luthor talks about
forming his own league, which can only be

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.199
the Injustice League.

00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:44.560
The group of villains was founded by Lex Luthor.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.060
At one point the team counted Deathstroke,
who makes a Justice League cameo, as one of

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.060
its members.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.699
At least 60 villains have cycled through the
Injustice League, but a few are already in

00:04:52.699 --> 00:04:54.000
the DCEU.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.900
Was that thief being apprehended by Wonder
Woman at the end of the film possibly Selina

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:58.900
Kyle?

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.340
Black Manta is scheduled to be a villain in
Aquaman's solo film, and half of the Suicide

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.810
Squad have been members, so that's a good
chunk of the Injustice League right there.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.210
Truth &amp; justice

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.889
Even the most casual fans of Superman know
that the big blue boy scout stands for three

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:12.889
main ideals:

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:16.780
"Truth, justice, and the American way."

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:21.110
Really old fans of Superman know that back
in the 1940s, Fleischer Studios' Superman

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.430
was only really into those first two ideals.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.490
"Superman fights for truth and justice!"

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:32.870
By the time the 1952 live-action TV series
came around, Superman was into 'the American

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:33.870
way' as well.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.220
The slogan even appears in 1978's Superman…

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.400
"I'm here to fight for truth, justice, and
the American way."

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.930
And of course, it even made an appearance
in Justice League, during Superman's fight

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:47.900
with Steppenwolf, when our reborn hero declares
that, in addition to truth, he's also kinda

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.530
into justice as well now.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:50.669
Good for him.

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:52.250
The original Jimmy Olsen

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:56.490
It's probably the most subtle Easter egg in
all of Justice League, but it's a good one.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.129
Most people probably won't recognize the officer
who checks Barry Allen into the prison to

00:06:00.129 --> 00:06:03.009
see his dad, but the actor's name is Marc
McClure.

00:06:03.009 --> 00:06:07.020
You may recognize him as Marty McFly's older
brother Dave, or, well…

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.400
"I was blown away when my dentist told me
I had tartar."

00:06:10.409 --> 00:06:14.729
Aside from that, McClure's most prominent
role was as newsboy Jimmy Olsen in all four

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:20.330
Christopher Reeve Superman films, and even
the wildly unpopular 1984 Supergirl movie,

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:24.819
making him the only actor to appear in more
Superman films than Christopher Reeve himself.

00:06:24.819 --> 00:06:26.410
Congrats on number six, Marc.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:27.410
Thanks for watching!

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.770
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:31.830
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

